  'The sequence begins with core concepts and skeptical pressure, then develops theories of justification, sources of knowledge, social knowledge, epistemic injustice, scientific inquiry, digital environments, and research writing.',
  $episjson${"slug": "epistemology", "title": "Epistemology", "description": "The sequence begins with core concepts and skeptical pressure, then develops theories of justification, sources of knowledge, social knowledge, epistemic injustice, scientific inquiry, digital environments, and research writing.", "topics": [{"id": "5f490f19-635b-42ab-8663-48dd53e07c73", "type": "topic", "title": "Epistemology as the Theory of Knowledge", "description": "Frame epistemology around knowledge, belief, truth, justification, rationality, evidence, inquiry, and intellectual responsibility.", "children": [{"id": "c5d0f665-2310-471c-bd95-e8463e2a54de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Central questions", "children": [{"id": "f1cdcac6-3a02-4beb-a733-0e8ba326f7b9", "type": "concept", "title": "What is knowledge?"}, {"id": "b0718216-e9a9-4db8-ba73-9bfc715e4fa5", "type": "concept", "title": "What makes belief rational?"}, {"id": "afdd0bea-d215-4bff-82f0-ca94999689e8", "type": "concept", "title": "What counts as evidence?"}, {"id": "43adc7c7-8b87-4bee-a710-8b2b716bf3b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Why do error and disagreement matter?"}], "description": "Students begin by seeing epistemology as the study of how beliefs can be true, justified, rational, responsible, or knowledge."}, {"id": "464693f2-4188-4b42-aee9-4c4ca66e1b62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Method in epistemology", "children": [{"id": "b00e922d-15d1-44d4-9642-0614de98afd2", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual analysis"}, {"id": "90ef8b0a-71c0-40ca-a086-a660b7b2a861", "type": "concept", "title": "Thought experiments"}, {"id": "fb115a1a-fb31-4eab-95bb-247a30eab2d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterexamples"}, {"id": "eb1537f3-3c4e-489c-bac5-68b92dd017a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflective equilibrium"}], "description": "The course uses argument analysis, cases, distinctions, and objections to clarify concepts."}, {"id": "bb8205b0-f402-41e6-9e3e-c6071452b8c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epistemology in life", "children": [{"id": "ff26db66-20f6-4b25-b356-82848e079cbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Expert trust"}, {"id": "9501bbbe-4a4e-406f-afd5-8fc2f306eec8", "type": "concept", "title": "Public disagreement"}, {"id": "b607f4c7-05cc-49c9-a75e-5b0f82798e8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation"}, {"id": "e9040973-5c77-4f61-8873-34de5f0b09be", "type": "concept", "title": "Intellectual responsibility"}], "description": "The field matters because inquiry, science, law, politics, education, and digital life depend on norms of belief."}]}, {"id": "382a426b-4c24-4235-9160-b12eafa67d52", "type": "topic", "title": "Knowledge, Belief, Truth, and the Value of Knowing", "description": "Analyze what distinguishes knowledge from true belief and why knowledge matters.", "children": [{"id": "60a57302-1737-4046-9876-ae060cf2b0e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Traditional analysis", "children": [{"id": "f36c4024-0549-4df6-9d49-9c63923ec6ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Belief condition"}, {"id": "b869ec06-f759-48c4-8e29-3bb4bef2d938", "type": "concept", "title": "Truth condition"}, {"id": "13019a47-598e-48ea-b923-b7799800540b", "type": "concept", "title": "Justification condition"}, {"id": "3c555328-59b8-490a-a78b-b76637cac1a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Sufficiency and necessity"}], "description": "The justified true belief model gives students a baseline for later debates."}, {"id": "2e58b6fd-9506-4b6b-b39c-952e1864899e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Truth and realism", "children": [{"id": "a4fb49e1-c93f-4104-99f2-cb133686719b", "type": "concept", "title": "Correspondence"}, {"id": "5007aea7-7969-4a48-9adf-51de7af6f748", "type": "concept", "title": "Coherence"}, {"id": "57730058-cb25-4592-8be9-cd20dbb2200d", "type": "concept", "title": "Pragmatic approaches"}, {"id": "99ad9e5c-6613-444f-8e79-d89a9f5f8c1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Realism and anti-realism"}], "description": "Knowledge requires truth, but philosophers dispute how truth should be understood."}, {"id": "d3ea1f43-23ba-455f-98d7-dd27bd124afb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Value of knowledge", "children": [{"id": "1a03feb5-b472-4e72-8f11-86274ed6ddb3", "type": "concept", "title": "Meno problem"}, {"id": "dc2f3f7d-9330-420a-aa8e-12b943a7db46", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical success"}, {"id": "563c6567-240e-4609-893d-4abdb38d3230", "type": "concept", "title": "Understanding"}, {"id": "bb7478f7-6f4b-4aa0-942a-70d83b7fbc46", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic achievement"}], "description": "Students ask why knowledge is more valuable than lucky true belief."}]}, {"id": "1c6ecd6b-7637-482e-93c7-aead6f05f1fb", "type": "topic", "title": "The Gettier Problem and Post-Gettier Epistemology", "description": "Use Gettier cases to test traditional analyses of knowledge and motivate contemporary theories.", "children": [{"id": "24255411-326d-4e89-8d0c-7707f345f9c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gettier cases", "children": [{"id": "c4ede446-c3a2-4844-a0e3-5dd8db2e89cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Luck"}, {"id": "748e3982-1a84-4d02-80a6-3cefdb0abec0", "type": "concept", "title": "False lemmas"}, {"id": "19544b1e-c2ed-4c6e-a1dc-24ffcb8a0ba1", "type": "concept", "title": "Coincidental truth"}, {"id": "75942e9c-c416-4a24-a77d-75bc9fec124a", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterexample method"}], "description": "Gettier examples show that justified true belief can still fall short of knowledge."}, {"id": "66b1fc16-5cf6-4afe-b589-4f16c1d4edce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "No-luck conditions", "children": [{"id": "4708e903-9181-4bce-9e7c-00c173de596a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensitivity"}, {"id": "d7b27f5b-b485-4062-90b1-6498de23d9b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Safety"}, {"id": "36a32279-8f5e-4b52-9f14-ab05bdf751fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Defeasibility"}, {"id": "f3f904fa-cd8b-4737-a99a-e1cae1f1918c", "type": "concept", "title": "Causal connections"}], "description": "Post-Gettier theories try to exclude epistemic luck without becoming too strict."}, {"id": "2d6f74e1-c5f1-42fe-958f-2c655169032f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Methodological lessons", "children": [{"id": "a5e419cb-d4dc-497e-a50b-604253bf8cac", "type": "concept", "title": "Case analysis"}, {"id": "6d8095da-6e76-4e1d-afa5-fbda7d2a776b", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory revision"}, {"id": "55c6853e-6f61-4700-9497-63d54101329e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intuition and its limits"}, {"id": "81996a6c-65f0-4d68-a6df-6fb101d902d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Philosophical progress"}], "description": "Students learn how a small case can reshape an entire theory."}]}, {"id": "0705da5e-dee9-48b0-852b-41ff0b445ff7", "type": "topic", "title": "Skepticism, Closure, and Responses to Radical Doubt", "description": "Study skeptical arguments and major strategies for resisting or living with them.", "children": [{"id": "1628c4de-9170-49f7-9191-1abc25c8ebf9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Skeptical arguments", "children": [{"id": "568d9053-164d-4f84-9d25-dbc5f4dc47d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Dream argument"}, {"id": "d2ed9080-92d1-466a-8c23-3659a7b0c6f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Evil demon"}, {"id": "8f4e5aae-eac7-4342-baae-6d535bea9390", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain in a vat"}, {"id": "68092809-3656-4cbf-9a48-fc80134ad342", "type": "concept", "title": "Cartesian doubt"}], "description": "Skepticism pressures ordinary claims to knowledge by raising error possibilities."}, {"id": "c56034a7-5522-41b8-9240-93b372749ba3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Closure and underdetermination", "children": [{"id": "8136a385-b34a-4689-ab3d-97ca8c7a9a5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Closure under known entailment"}, {"id": "aa7c2b1e-ae9a-4b2b-93db-1177baa50667", "type": "concept", "title": "Underdetermination"}, {"id": "81465900-a168-4bbb-8290-851a91c9d76e", "type": "concept", "title": "Tracking alternatives"}, {"id": "71415712-b8d5-4a26-9000-8c9504debeb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Relevant alternatives"}], "description": "These principles explain why skepticism can feel hard to escape."}, {"id": "ff9f558c-f0f4-4984-b04e-ec5b6c022aa4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Responses to skepticism", "children": [{"id": "0f671ca6-abf7-4edc-a918-6a4159f7d3e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Moorean response"}, {"id": "fa656dbe-2572-4591-8c19-c7477c16d241", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextualism"}, {"id": "a0a5e51a-dbc0-48c7-ab84-65293139492b", "type": "concept", "title": "Externalist response"}, {"id": "2ca2802c-f5b1-481e-9fa7-34038897aeba", "type": "concept", "title": "Hinge epistemology"}], "description": "Students compare strategies for resisting, conceding, or reframing skeptical challenges."}]}, {"id": "ab400afe-e974-4660-9b8e-7e41dde205d2", "type": "topic", "title": "Foundationalism, Coherentism, and Infinitism", "description": "Compare structural theories of justification and the regress problems they answer.", "children": [{"id": "398fb4bb-0521-4dbc-84ba-6d9b0dcc48df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regress problem", "children": [{"id": "11545e19-eb20-47da-a7ad-868251f9faa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Infinite regress"}, {"id": "c03c60cd-6120-45ae-86d1-8ed714edebb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Circularity"}, {"id": "dbb060e1-400b-43db-b24b-d271ea72b45a", "type": "concept", "title": "Arbitrary stopping"}, {"id": "fb2bfc3a-da29-48ca-9453-9486dcb11159", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic support"}], "description": "The regress problem asks how chains of reasons can end, circle, or continue."}, {"id": "f2f4b63d-7227-4149-a4b8-3d3399081e44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Foundationalism", "children": [{"id": "2fd67b7e-e73f-4a5e-b45e-4f0c6348e4fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Basic beliefs"}, {"id": "412fa7da-cd6c-4811-b6fc-a938276abe86", "type": "concept", "title": "Given and noninferential justification"}, {"id": "d62d0ac6-54c8-419c-b406-a8f064da4163", "type": "concept", "title": "Perceptual foundations"}, {"id": "f38d516a-083b-4153-9303-9ed86d41b5b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Classical and modest versions"}], "description": "Foundationalism claims some beliefs are justified without depending on other beliefs."}, {"id": "1538bef7-ae13-4000-9a74-0c556464d11d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Coherentism and infinitism", "children": [{"id": "92e3b68e-a939-4e32-986d-d31902318e69", "type": "concept", "title": "Explanatory coherence"}, {"id": "ef4a2ebf-c05e-4ce7-8625-e23dfbeb665e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutual support"}, {"id": "51169dc3-83bb-447a-b6a4-773fe804097b", "type": "concept", "title": "Linear chains"}, {"id": "ea756569-b6d0-4d00-8a62-7c2dd143bf12", "type": "concept", "title": "Objections and advantages"}], "description": "Alternative structures explain justification through networks or endless reasons."}]}, {"id": "5b9d17d5-9807-4563-a2d0-a297b11a11e0", "type": "topic", "title": "Internalism, Externalism, Reliabilism, and Proper Function", "description": "Examine whether justification depends on reflective access, reliable process, or cognitive design.", "children": [{"id": "f5e7c6ea-b654-41b3-b888-5e70a16af5d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Internalism", "children": [{"id": "158d2a5b-6543-4f08-9bb6-136520d4463b", "type": "concept", "title": "Access internalism"}, {"id": "b21db128-b0d6-4e8e-a5dc-2de35c4ef950", "type": "concept", "title": "Mentalism"}, {"id": "ccf0ff28-1c67-4b6f-a62f-b4b2fc4289ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Reasons and responsibility"}, {"id": "7f576689-db85-46c2-b894-0bf98ca02065", "type": "concept", "title": "New evil demon problem"}], "description": "Internalists emphasize what a subject can access or reflectively appreciate."}, {"id": "cdd2d6a3-7be1-4d5d-9f5c-ea6050e198ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reliabilism", "children": [{"id": "3f539ee9-4300-434d-8f5c-4876114a670d", "type": "concept", "title": "Process reliability"}, {"id": "f0122149-78bc-4b1f-85c1-a0e6845204e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Perception and memory"}, {"id": "884cc6ce-d1f3-4959-8090-f324e8f54556", "type": "concept", "title": "Generality problem"}, {"id": "9704106b-c7ca-4a66-85a8-f6aff06c00ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability versus reflection"}], "description": "Reliabilism links justification or knowledge to reliable belief-forming processes."}, {"id": "b963f2d6-1b6c-4539-b6e8-1df380bf8cf3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Proper function", "children": [{"id": "0d29819d-2cb0-4437-b0af-b4d6f6ce89d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Design plan"}, {"id": "f5d71b9a-0e3c-44ef-af42-6460a0d70864", "type": "concept", "title": "Apt environment"}, {"id": "690ecaaf-d819-459b-9795-17db89b45350", "type": "concept", "title": "Warrant"}, {"id": "7e98ca43-c3f1-472a-9c01-f65f5f03939c", "type": "concept", "title": "Naturalized epistemology"}], "description": "Proper function accounts connect knowledge to cognitive design, environment, and successful functioning."}]}, {"id": "564c29d3-7513-489c-89a0-d54a4c5f0d96", "type": "topic", "title": "A Priori Knowledge, Rational Insight, and Analyticity", "description": "Investigate knowledge that seems independent of experience, including logic, mathematics, and conceptual truth.", "children": [{"id": "b978e429-bf01-4b2a-93fd-336abf6efe13", "type": "subtopic", "title": "A priori and a posteriori", "children": [{"id": "d796a820-a72a-4bc9-b45a-a17e3286ce3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Definitions"}, {"id": "4efb252c-63fe-4465-a7c6-04942e4f7876", "type": "concept", "title": "Mathematics"}, {"id": "147b438f-2dd7-4d1e-a802-ebce15909d47", "type": "concept", "title": "Logic"}, {"id": "a4a12216-8307-4095-83c5-7baae4398ee8", "type": "concept", "title": "Modal knowledge"}], "description": "Students learn to distinguish experience-based knowledge from knowledge apparently independent of experience."}, {"id": "46341f12-d5eb-4e49-8127-83f54dfc9c37", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Analyticity and necessity", "children": [{"id": "9c41def1-5fae-41d7-9a12-b40be154986c", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytic and synthetic"}, {"id": "5b0fe2fc-25b3-4ff9-9805-3aae8a1461a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Necessary and contingent"}, {"id": "65a8cf56-5268-4f5c-843b-dfbd27c0e0c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Kripkean complications"}, {"id": "30d35183-0e1b-4f52-90e5-43654d1369d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual truth"}], "description": "The topic connects language, concepts, and necessity."}, {"id": "3c459719-fef2-45e9-a836-7a398c654e96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kant and rational insight", "children": [{"id": "d35b3321-fd42-4267-a138-10da640c3870", "type": "concept", "title": "Synthetic a priori"}, {"id": "1308341b-17dc-428f-aa8a-1a23d65b74a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Conditions of experience"}, {"id": "b3730290-c614-4cdd-b2d6-1576aea69843", "type": "concept", "title": "Space and time"}, {"id": "7c02c627-bc05-4b63-9f6c-18ae91d6fd92", "type": "concept", "title": "Categories"}], "description": "Kant's synthetic a priori offers a historical and systematic challenge."}]}, {"id": "b1743a5f-7870-4177-ba6f-e33d2a022ddb", "type": "topic", "title": "Perception, Memory, and Introspection", "description": "Assess major sources of belief and their vulnerabilities to error, illusion, and self-deception.", "children": [{"id": "0e5661ed-1d17-45b5-a91c-99849fe1931a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Perception", "children": [{"id": "c13d48ea-0e73-484d-8903-570153278c59", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct realism"}, {"id": "bc0f20be-675d-4e5d-a1ca-1409d6757de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Representationalism"}, {"id": "3490281d-454a-44f4-ab55-5f55ec4d425a", "type": "concept", "title": "Illusion"}, {"id": "c128faec-8ffe-44d4-8481-b5e35b47208b", "type": "concept", "title": "Hallucination"}], "description": "Perceptual knowledge is central because most empirical knowledge depends on the world appearing to us."}, {"id": "5eb2d5cf-7345-435f-86d0-509e11d582e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory", "children": [{"id": "e353f406-22ab-4381-b412-cba1097537d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Episodic memory"}, {"id": "95b66c25-2c74-4b2e-b102-33359a56f82a", "type": "concept", "title": "Semantic memory"}, {"id": "406f7b0b-f295-454a-b6d2-64aaf80423e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory reliability"}, {"id": "51fd4735-0444-4cfc-b76c-8a4ccd499c05", "type": "concept", "title": "Forgetting and distortion"}], "description": "Memory raises questions about preservation, reconstruction, and justification over time."}, {"id": "b1e9adc0-ed9b-4865-8a7b-40980af2d9b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Introspection", "children": [{"id": "e7a10c3a-0d08-4ce9-b27e-5fb10b7cf43a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mental states"}, {"id": "dfff4f76-539f-4de9-8350-3280c0063396", "type": "concept", "title": "Transparency"}, {"id": "a24e8ab2-4051-43e1-af57-99486c09f40d", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-deception"}, {"id": "d254b6fa-2dfa-4a6c-b7bb-8d15ff2f2493", "type": "concept", "title": "First-person authority"}], "description": "Self-knowledge can seem privileged, yet it is vulnerable to error."}]}, {"id": "8ac5e50a-35a6-4100-b406-baf26a1aa38c", "type": "topic", "title": "Induction, Probability, Evidence, and Bayesian Reasoning", "description": "Connect classical problems of induction to evidence, confirmation, probability, and rational belief update.", "children": [{"id": "1f65f9ab-046e-4c7b-862b-0f34a03af095", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Problem of induction", "children": [{"id": "3fe63da3-aa54-4a1e-93d0-4729c74a4003", "type": "concept", "title": "Uniformity of nature"}, {"id": "2cfee203-de8c-4ffe-9cd6-52e6178de49f", "type": "concept", "title": "Circular justification"}, {"id": "62f2ee8b-fea0-4b23-b7ab-458ba6352430", "type": "concept", "title": "Causation"}, {"id": "71d04abb-974b-418a-97d5-7a1fa1c14968", "type": "concept", "title": "Naturalized response"}], "description": "Hume's problem asks why past regularities justify expectations about the future."}, {"id": "4b98f49a-e79e-4be5-a6fa-59a1ae1a2337", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confirmation and evidence", "children": [{"id": "e0d9dff7-505b-4e18-b904-6cdad2701e89", "type": "concept", "title": "Hypothesis testing"}, {"id": "27754c04-b3a5-4720-91e2-601d9b52e2e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Confirmation"}, {"id": "76d88663-5d30-4238-ad50-4de0473c92ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Underdetermination"}, {"id": "34cf5718-7bf4-4c93-b7b1-18159d7981ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Inference to the best explanation"}], "description": "Students examine what it means for evidence to support a hypothesis."}, {"id": "26c72416-75fc-484e-a817-06d3b6358e14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bayesian reasoning", "children": [{"id": "18a453b1-4708-498a-8cce-36b5ebd1b252", "type": "concept", "title": "Priors"}, {"id": "da23865a-9098-42d3-9183-84bb10922a2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Likelihoods"}, {"id": "6747a25a-5455-4ec5-be65-0294cb07c245", "type": "concept", "title": "Posterior probability"}, {"id": "dd34b780-a028-42f6-b46e-99df0135b22b", "type": "concept", "title": "Base-rate neglect"}], "description": "Bayesian tools clarify rational updating and the role of prior belief."}]}, {"id": "5f652b80-7c3b-4d67-9d67-2427dcb06bb3", "type": "topic", "title": "Testimony, Trust, Experts, and Disagreement", "description": "Study how knowledge depends on other people, institutions, expertise, and disagreement.", "children": [{"id": "da025707-27ac-493b-810c-41180dd437eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Testimony", "children": [{"id": "b3998ab2-8aa4-416a-8649-aea334b27254", "type": "concept", "title": "Reductionism"}, {"id": "754a13dc-6e2c-45d1-8d4c-517743d09ec7", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-reductionism"}, {"id": "41e4a644-a8da-471d-aa4e-bbedb9a32d3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker reliability"}, {"id": "87a7bd78-9b4c-4227-8bb3-ebf9669e39a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Hearer responsibility"}], "description": "Much of what we know depends on others, so testimony is not a marginal source."}, {"id": "429b9ba9-ae22-4930-b1e2-36e556afc747", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Expertise", "children": [{"id": "2d096d85-2278-41d6-b391-ec2943c0ddf7", "type": "concept", "title": "Credentials"}, {"id": "5d208cd3-cb8b-49a8-b035-3b34665ca71b", "type": "concept", "title": "Track record"}, {"id": "3ef02580-c99f-4244-a0a8-a478bbdd813f", "type": "concept", "title": "Consensus"}, {"id": "c85831cc-5f01-40d4-9d5f-37a25cdf51db", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflicts of interest"}], "description": "Students learn how to assess expert authority without pretending to become experts in everything."}, {"id": "d0cb49e7-4ff4-47d8-abc6-cc2ea80a286b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Disagreement", "children": [{"id": "73f1981d-16d6-4079-84d8-a28d8c9ae735", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer disagreement"}, {"id": "b043db6d-0f31-421f-8e9a-67668569870d", "type": "concept", "title": "Conciliationism"}, {"id": "ad2bc636-4fa9-4d12-9070-e5d71d822dca", "type": "concept", "title": "Steadfastness"}, {"id": "299566d7-7266-4535-a1d0-af050845da98", "type": "concept", "title": "Deep disagreement"}], "description": "Disagreement pressures confidence and intellectual humility."}]}, {"id": "74ef98d1-9805-4508-b0c5-381fe3f3247c", "type": "topic", "title": "Social Epistemology and Collective Knowledge", "description": "Understand how groups, communities, media, science, and institutions shape knowledge production.", "children": [{"id": "6ad36462-31d4-4f7a-8cf2-2fd3a11a4e70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Knowledge communities", "children": [{"id": "b97ac6b7-f57c-4314-8d5f-cfa01428c868", "type": "concept", "title": "Division of cognitive labor"}, {"id": "87e20c5d-3162-4009-9149-e48c42391a43", "type": "concept", "title": "Scientific communities"}, {"id": "0cfb7b32-5583-4ab8-93a6-7f858eff2b58", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer review"}, {"id": "b7ca8594-1d32-4ada-bd01-dec9b580b72c", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional trust"}], "description": "Knowledge is often produced by groups, not isolated thinkers."}, {"id": "dfd98220-dc97-4d58-8fe3-ef93be225c40", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collective agents", "children": [{"id": "a4cfc8d1-ef47-4230-b9ad-6496cea0035a", "type": "concept", "title": "Group belief"}, {"id": "91688717-d0bd-4f62-9c78-8a51ae7e41b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective evidence"}, {"id": "80748977-51b9-4569-85ee-993f44d23e67", "type": "concept", "title": "Distributed cognition"}, {"id": "24c5cc5f-4ad8-4f30-98a3-91de8ab9af67", "type": "concept", "title": "Organizational ignorance"}], "description": "Students ask whether groups can believe, know, or be responsible."}, {"id": "310c72e2-ea59-4c17-a64f-7be6d2e274aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Information environments", "children": [{"id": "ee65b91d-6de8-437d-a21b-906e843fe653", "type": "concept", "title": "Media systems"}, {"id": "00913018-2fa2-4980-8764-02d9d0da834a", "type": "concept", "title": "Echo chambers"}, {"id": "87036072-0c0c-4119-aa77-c07f0edcfd85", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic bubbles"}, {"id": "8d9275a2-c836-480d-9f9f-17cb758da5a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Public knowledge"}], "description": "Social structures affect which beliefs spread and which evidence becomes visible."}]}, {"id": "c6d6c07b-5a06-4d59-8d54-0f674abccc7b", "type": "topic", "title": "Epistemic Injustice, Standpoint, and Power", "description": "Analyze how social power can distort credibility, interpretation, participation, and access to knowledge.", "children": [{"id": "263f20c3-c0cc-4246-a6cc-715a44793a86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Testimonial injustice", "children": [{"id": "f0d9e902-2552-49f8-9aa2-4db4762c9829", "type": "concept", "title": "Credibility deficit"}, {"id": "304b2baa-41ec-4227-b509-6af8ba075a37", "type": "concept", "title": "Credibility excess"}, {"id": "c54e89eb-4846-4a94-b8ec-049f7a34e725", "type": "concept", "title": "Prejudice"}, {"id": "2170a867-c49b-4a9c-9b25-73de8b04e63c", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker silencing"}], "description": "Students analyze how credibility can be unfairly inflated or deflated."}, {"id": "93f4926e-419f-471a-8ffd-6e0c896990a1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hermeneutical injustice", "children": [{"id": "f0dc6af0-697d-4f25-a961-e0557885ebce", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretive gaps"}, {"id": "5a6fa530-8034-4224-b08d-f582e24b0617", "type": "concept", "title": "Marginalization"}, {"id": "ee1f245f-45a0-4aba-8d97-c855dd460962", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept creation"}, {"id": "370bc9e0-c561-43a6-860c-928eb0aea2c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Social uptake"}], "description": "Some experiences become hard to understand because shared interpretive resources are missing."}, {"id": "e3170df0-b57d-492d-a164-47d735bea823", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Standpoint and situated knowledge", "children": [{"id": "34221cb9-3c2d-4397-9173-f2b5374c2e0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Standpoint epistemology"}, {"id": "a499f91c-1eaf-4f84-b1e3-bc3df7e6c63b", "type": "concept", "title": "Strong objectivity"}, {"id": "4c7ee5fa-9954-4b6d-8ac6-614d55efea73", "type": "concept", "title": "Experience and evidence"}, {"id": "823c2fe1-6a64-4b67-81fa-b132d152d4c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Power and ignorance"}], "description": "The course connects social position to evidence, inquiry, and objectivity."}]}, {"id": "1d45c64c-5771-4ffc-bc6b-682127adc9ac", "type": "topic", "title": "Virtue Epistemology, Intellectual Character, and Inquiry", "description": "Connect knowledge to intellectual virtues, vices, responsibility, curiosity, humility, and courage.", "children": [{"id": "78e7dd73-121e-4b54-b622-bc71950569a6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epistemic virtues", "children": [{"id": "dd197205-c0ad-47c6-aa3c-c3f9ee3c3883", "type": "concept", "title": "Open-mindedness"}, {"id": "913fd80f-2c6d-4329-adeb-b5533f444198", "type": "concept", "title": "Intellectual humility"}, {"id": "01a0f129-7e20-4383-a61a-edc18569785b", "type": "concept", "title": "Courage"}, {"id": "8f1f606f-b6d7-429f-8417-2d7c013f7d22", "type": "concept", "title": "Carefulness"}], "description": "Virtue epistemology focuses on the character traits and abilities of good inquirers."}, {"id": "fca28653-069a-4351-82b0-d0b10fbebcef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epistemic vices", "children": [{"id": "32487748-f5b5-4406-bd15-fa640866d4cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Dogmatism"}, {"id": "4c43bae4-0a2f-40ef-968b-7fd0cf57d8a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Closed-mindedness"}, {"id": "ad342ada-903c-4bde-b50a-612182e092b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Arrogance"}, {"id": "bcd7198d-e867-4f2b-b0d9-e9a0473b9f15", "type": "concept", "title": "Wishful thinking"}], "description": "Students examine how character flaws and habits damage inquiry."}, {"id": "cd77e8f3-bdb2-41ef-bf6e-29cc9c575232", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Credit and achievement", "children": [{"id": "b19de977-30df-475d-a2c7-10e53e035d38", "type": "concept", "title": "Apt belief"}, {"id": "d248a0b9-6fdd-48df-b044-0b0453a6b969", "type": "concept", "title": "Skill"}, {"id": "a7cce93f-8050-40e0-a932-8d18a49d36ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Credit"}, {"id": "9c8b0b39-293e-41f1-ae6e-a6a69d69bb34", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsibility"}], "description": "Some theories explain knowledge as a kind of successful cognitive achievement."}]}, {"id": "9cc65585-90f6-4058-b809-9b06c493b2a4", "type": "topic", "title": "Epistemology of Science, Models, and Explanation", "description": "Apply epistemological tools to scientific evidence, models, idealization, realism, and values in inquiry.", "children": [{"id": "4f8cb7cc-6b13-465b-ae49-8e5ef60632a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific evidence", "children": [{"id": "f882da31-bf0c-444e-aadb-c523c43228cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation"}, {"id": "fa23a604-6fb9-4464-87a0-c04b91813288", "type": "concept", "title": "Experiment"}, {"id": "d5ac380a-8ef3-4d35-a116-f675dbdc5d48", "type": "concept", "title": "Replication"}, {"id": "53576ce3-1871-4ac3-90d4-8da33281ca60", "type": "concept", "title": "Measurement error"}], "description": "Science raises special questions about observation, experiment, measurement, and theory."}, {"id": "e0aefadd-c655-475e-bc86-ddcec50869c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models and explanation", "children": [{"id": "fcde5fa7-9d76-4d28-ad36-1ea4d0eb7471", "type": "concept", "title": "Idealization"}, {"id": "532778e4-285d-46a2-b0bc-2ed6f87f341b", "type": "concept", "title": "Abstraction"}, {"id": "59d1e0a7-620b-40cc-bf02-7d66daa8858b", "type": "concept", "title": "Explanation"}, {"id": "09705870-eb49-4d10-ab1b-41b0c3d77891", "type": "concept", "title": "Prediction"}], "description": "Models can be useful even when idealized or false in detail."}, {"id": "f80ea817-0734-466c-8fc5-f660b2f2ea1f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Realism and values", "children": [{"id": "3f491446-65ca-4f5a-9038-6060ea39fcb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scientific realism"}, {"id": "a2ece88b-7241-4f3c-a9aa-c2d78027032f", "type": "concept", "title": "Constructive empiricism"}, {"id": "4ba28e5b-28a6-4a6e-9302-fd316292b3f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Underdetermination"}, {"id": "d0082270-94d6-456c-8710-a55ebfe66d97", "type": "concept", "title": "Values in science"}], "description": "Students ask whether science reveals reality and how values enter inquiry."}]}, {"id": "2821eb6b-3984-4516-954e-9df3b9dd1aed", "type": "topic", "title": "Digital Epistemology, Misinformation, and AI-Mediated Knowledge", "description": "Extend epistemology to search, platforms, algorithms, data, misinformation, and AI systems.", "children": [{"id": "e6f13bfc-25c3-47a3-b98c-c970a4d67424", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Online knowledge", "children": [{"id": "3e251d52-d934-48f9-8bf4-2bcdfc1e2ccf", "type": "concept", "title": "Search engines"}, {"id": "369365bc-34f6-4ae5-bb2a-5531ec205e69", "type": "concept", "title": "Platforms"}, {"id": "d171c1c3-d1c1-496a-a308-4a390cca30e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Virality"}, {"id": "9cc59657-f585-40fe-b2ca-5f73652ba1e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Source evaluation"}], "description": "Digital environments reshape evidence, authority, memory, and trust."}, {"id": "a7e2863b-4098-4ae6-95ee-2224e2a9ac99", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Misinformation", "children": [{"id": "d042b519-ff67-4a21-a26d-650faa4f18f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Disinformation"}, {"id": "a59ed1ab-e446-423c-b617-f7d89257030a", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation"}, {"id": "f7f36880-9b5f-4e8d-bf18-95749f96ba3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conspiracy theories"}, {"id": "a98989a5-cc0f-4003-b781-98d69c8f1841", "type": "concept", "title": "Correction and backfire effects"}], "description": "Students apply epistemology to false belief formation at scale."}, {"id": "a1353c18-0924-48f6-bfa8-01e8f77c826f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AI and algorithms", "children": [{"id": "e16aebf8-4796-4c7c-9f69-e181ff8e14a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithmic authority"}, {"id": "24016078-c4dc-4349-aa2d-1982d14bd5ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Explainability"}, {"id": "56817d12-ad1f-4a0b-bcb3-476ee9ba8961", "type": "concept", "title": "Hallucination"}, {"id": "3e055891-d8ac-4916-a0b5-81445cc0df5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Human oversight"}], "description": "AI systems create new questions about testimony, opacity, and epistemic dependence."}]}, {"id": "627587da-1957-4855-a637-26e3255e828f", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone Research Paper on Justification and Knowledge", "description": "Develop a rigorous philosophical research paper with argument reconstruction, objection, reply, and scholarly sources.", "children": [{"id": "03584df8-d634-497e-9fa3-a07b4f8fd620", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question", "children": [{"id": "b771c9c3-019b-4482-bfec-d389d8c3c84a", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic narrowing"}, {"id": "9da2e9fe-98ec-4c39-8123-e16a29eafb38", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis formation"}, {"id": "ead228ae-b3a3-4fd5-a9b0-39b186b54e8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Literature search"}, {"id": "a759926d-2a36-4f73-a26b-e75082d98fa2", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument map"}], "description": "A strong paper begins with a focused question that matters to a live debate."}, {"id": "91617475-720e-42f0-b019-5ee8e75d46e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Argument reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "f55bc5c8-85fb-4314-862c-e8c9afaaa3f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Premises"}, {"id": "a60c8754-7c42-4e4b-b096-06d7f99aca41", "type": "concept", "title": "Conclusion"}, {"id": "1355a615-741f-4b7a-a77d-246cd806bff5", "type": "concept", "title": "Hidden assumptions"}, {"id": "9c3844f8-a84a-420a-bec5-fc667d07d854", "type": "concept", "title": "Charitable interpretation"}], "description": "Students practice reconstructing arguments before criticizing them."}, {"id": "917b1117-e318-434b-af43-aa8cfb224e0b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Objection and reply", "children": [{"id": "880e9d87-81aa-4ce2-adbe-b41ae555a02c", "type": "concept", "title": "Original objection"}, {"id": "c0b745a3-2159-4749-a457-d334a733e836", "type": "concept", "title": "Reply"}, {"id": "164664a1-70c9-45f0-866a-8c97ecc02261", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision"}, {"id": "b05f5fea-ea76-4280-b727-63959acf3cdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly citation"}], "description": "The final paper develops philosophical independence through objections and replies."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$episjson$::jsonb,
